# od — real-world examples

## Dump a file in octal format

```sh
od /path/to/binaryfile
od -o /path/to/binaryfile
od -t o2 /path/to/binaryfile
```

## Dump a file in hexadecimal format

```sh
od -x /path/to/binaryfile
od -t x2 /path/to/binaryfile
```

## Hexadecimal with hex offsets and one byte per column

```sh
od -A x -t x1 /path/to/binaryfile
```

## Homelab: check a file's magic bytes

```sh
# ELF binaries start with 7f 45 4c 46 ("ELF")
od -A x -t x1z -N 8 /usr/bin/ls
```

## View characters and escape sequences

```sh
# See tabs, newlines, CRLF line endings in a config file
od -c ~/.config/somefile | head
```

| Flag  | Meaning                                     |
| ----- | ------------------------------------------- |
| -t x1 | output type: hex, 1 byte per column         |
| -A x  | show offsets in hex (o=octal, d=decimal)    |
| -N    | dump only the first N bytes                 |
| -j    | skip N bytes before dumping                 |
| -c    | printable characters and backslash escapes  |
| -z    | append printable-character column (with -t) |
